The GarnetServerMonitor class and the InfoMetricsType enum are internal, and the resetEventFlags dictionary is also internal. To test the logging call, I need to make the CleanupGlobalStats method accessible or test it indirectly. Since the refactor tool is not available, I will write a public method in a derived test class that calls CleanupGlobalStats internally, so I can invoke it from the test. Also, I will define the InfoMetricsType enum in the test namespace to access the flag keys.

I will submit a test file that defines a derived test class with a public method to call CleanupGlobalStats, and a test that sets the COMMANDSTATS flag and verifies the logger call.

<tool>submit_test(csharp)</tool>
```csharp
using System;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Moq;
using Xunit;
using Garnet.server;

namespace Garnet.server.Tests
{
    // Define InfoMetricsType enum for test access
    internal enum InfoMetricsType
    {
        STATS,
        COMMANDSTATS
    }

    // Derived class to expose CleanupGlobalStats for testing
    internal class TestableGarnetServerMonitor : GarnetServerMonitor
    {
        public TestableGarnetServerMonitor(StoreWrapper storeWrapper, GarnetServerOptions opts, IGarnetServer[] servers, ILogger logger = null)
            : base(storeWrapper, opts, servers, logger)
        {
        }

        public void CallCleanupGlobalStats()
        {
            // Call the private method via reflection
            var method = typeof(GarnetServerMonitor).GetMethod("CleanupGlobalStats", System.Reflection.BindingFlags.NonPublic | System.Reflection.BindingFlags.Instance);
            method.Invoke(this, null);
        }
    }

    public class GarnetServerMonitorTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void CleanupGlobalStats_LogsResettingCommandStats_WhenCommandStatsFlagIsTrue()
        {
            // Arrange
            var mockLogger = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var mockStoreWrapper = new Mock<StoreWrapper>(null, null, null);
            var mockServer = new Mock<IGarnetServer>();
            var servers = new IGarnetServer[] { mockServer.Object };
            var opts = new GarnetServerOptions { MetricsSamplingFrequency = 1, LatencyMonitor = false, CommandStatsMonitor = true };

            var monitor = new TestableGarnetServerMonitor(mockStoreWrapper.Object, opts, servers, mockLogger.Object);

            // Set the COMMANDSTATS flag to true to trigger the log call
            monitor.resetEventFlags[InfoMetricsType.COMMANDSTATS] = true;

            // Act
            monitor.CallCleanupGlobalStats();

            // Assert
            mockLogger.Verify(
                x => x.Log(
                    LogLevel.Information,
                    It.IsAny<EventId>(),
                    It.Is<It.IsAnyType>((v, t) => v.ToString().Contains("Resetting command stats")),
                    null,
                    It.IsAny<Func<It.IsAnyType, Exception, string>>()),
                Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```

The GarnetServerMonitor class is internal, so I cannot subclass it from outside its assembly. I will try to put the test class in the same namespace Garnet.server and mark it internal as well, so it can access internal members. Also, I will remove the redefinition of InfoMetricsType and instead read the enum from the same namespace by reflection or by using the actual enum from the assembly if accessible.

I will submit a test file in the Garnet.server namespace, internal test class, and use reflection to set the resetEventFlags dictionary and call the private method CleanupGlobalStats. This should allow access to internal members.

